msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introdución"

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk6 trunk5 stable6
#: index.docbook:32
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
#| msgid "In many small local area networks, the <acronym>SMB</acronym> protocol is used to offer network services. Names like <quote>&Windows; Network</quote> or <quote>&Windows; for Workgroups Network</quote> or <quote>LanManager</quote> are often used as well. Using <acronym>SMB</acronym> you can access so-called <quote>shares</quote> (&ie; folders made available by the server) as well as printers."
msgid "In many small local area networks, the &SMB; protocol is used to offer network services. Names like <quote>&Windows; Network</quote> or <quote>&Windows; for Workgroups Network</quote> or <quote>LanManager</quote> are often used as well. Using &SMB; you can access so-called <quote>shares</quote> (&ie; folders made available by the server) as well as printers."
msgstr "En moitas redes de área local pequenas úsase o protocolo <acronym>SMB</acronym> para ofrecer servizos de rede, aínda que en ocasións cos nomes <quote>Rede de &Windows;</quote> ou <quote>Rede de &Windows; para grupos de traballo</quote> ou <quote>LanManager</quote>. Mediante <acronym>SMB</acronym> pode acceder ás chamadas <quote>comparticións</quote> (&ie; cartafoles que o servidor pon á disposición) e tamén a impresoras."

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk6 trunk5 stable6
#: index.docbook:40
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
#| msgid "&kde; comes with built-in support for the <acronym>SMB</acronym> protocol. As &kde; is network-transparent that means you can access <acronym>SMB</acronym> shares from everywhere you can access your local files, for example in the &konqueror; file manager and in the file dialog. To make use of this you should provide &kde; with some information on your <acronym>SMB</acronym> network. But don't worry, this is normally pretty simple as, for example, all the Windows clients in your network need and have the same information."
msgid "&kde; comes with built-in support for the &SMB; protocol. As &kde; is network-transparent that means you can access &SMB; shares from everywhere you can access your local files, for example in the &konqueror; file manager and in the file dialog. To make use of this you should provide &kde; with some information on your &SMB; network. But don't worry, this is normally pretty simple as, for example, all the &Windows; clients in your network need and have the same information."
msgstr "&kde; ten de seu compatibilidade co protocolo <acronym>SMB</acronym>, e xa que é transparente á rede pode acceder a comparticións <acronym>SMB</acronym> desde calquera lugar que poida acceder aos ficheiros locais, por exemplo no xestor de ficheiros &konqueror; e no diálogo de ficheiros. Para usar isto debe fornecerlle informacións a &kde; sobre a rede <acronym>SMB</acronym>; non se preocupe, xeralmente isto é moi sinxelo xa que, por exemplo, todos os clientes de Windows na rede precisan e teñen a mesma información."

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk6 trunk5 stable6
#: index.docbook:58
#, no-c-format
msgid "For the &SMB; protocol to work, it is required to have &Samba; correctly installed."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk6 trunk5 stable6
#: index.docbook:67
#, no-c-format
msgid "<title>Use</title>"
msgstr "<title>Utilización</title>"

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk6 trunk5 stable6
#: index.docbook:69
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
#| msgid "Although there are a lot of insecure <acronym>SMB</acronym> networks out there which allow access to anyone, in principle you have to authenticate yourself to access the services of an <acronym>SMB</acronym> server. By default, &kde; will use the data entered in the <guilabel>Default user name</guilabel> and <guilabel>Default password</guilabel> fields to authenticate itself on <acronym>SMB</acronym> hosts. If you leave the field <guilabel>Default user name</guilabel> empty, &kde; will try to access <acronym>SMB</acronym> hosts without a username. If you leave the default password empty, it will try without a password. If &kde; is unsuccessful accessing the host using these settings, you will be asked for a username and a password."
msgid "Although there are a lot of insecure &SMB; networks out there which allow access to anyone, in principle you have to authenticate yourself to access the services of an &SMB; server. By default, &kde; will use the data entered in the <guilabel>Default user name:</guilabel> and <guilabel>Default password:</guilabel> fields to authenticate itself on &SMB; hosts. If you leave the field <guilabel>Default user name:</guilabel> empty, &kde; will try to access &SMB; hosts without a username. If you leave the default password empty, it will try without a password. If &kde; is unsuccessful accessing the host using these settings, you will be asked for a username and a password."
msgstr "Aínda que hai moitas redes <acronym>SMB</acronym> inseguras que permiten o acceso a calquera, en principio ten que autenticarse para acceder aos servizos do servidor <acronym>SMB</acronym>. De maneira predeterminada &kde; ha usar os datos inseridos nos campos <guilabel>Nome do usuario predeterminado</guilabel> e <guilabel>Contrasinal predeterminado</guilabel> para autenticarse nos servidores <acronym>SMB</acronym>. Se deixa o primeiro deses campos baleiro &kde; ha intentar acceder ao servidor <acronym>SMB</acronym> sen usar contrasinal. Se o acceso non tivese éxito así, preguntaralle polo nome de usuaria e o contrasinal."

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk6 trunk5 stable6
#: index.docbook:82
#, fuzzy, no-c-format
#| msgid "While it makes things more comfortable if &kde; stores your <acronym>SMB</acronym> password, this may be a security problem. If you are using <acronym>SMB</acronym> in a security conscious environment, you should not store your password here but rather enter it anew every time you need to access an <acronym>SMB</acronym> host."
msgid "While it makes things more comfortable if &kde; stores your &SMB; password, this may be a security problem. If you are using &SMB; in a security conscious environment, you should not store your password here but rather enter it anew every time you need to access an &SMB; host."
msgstr "Aínda que é máis cómodo que &kde; garde o contrasinal <acronym>SMB</acronym>, isto é un problema de seguridade. Se está a usar <acronym>SMB</acronym> nun ambiente con conciencia pola seguridade non debera gardalo senón inserilo cada vez que precise acceso a un servidor <acronym>SMB</acronym>."
